      "text": "To find the length \\( QS \\), we will use the given information and some trigonometric properties. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - \\(\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\\)\n   - \\( QR = 9 \\)\n\n2. **Understand the Right Triangle:**\n   Since \\( \\cos R = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\cos R = \\frac{QR}{QR + SR} = \\frac{9}{QR + SR} = \\frac{4}{9}\n   \\]\n   Given \\( QR = 9 \\), we can set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{9}{9 + SR} = \\frac{4}{9}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\( SR \\):**\n   \\[\n   9 \\cdot 9 = 4 \\cdot (9 + SR)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   81 = 36 + 4SR\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4SR = 45\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   SR = \\frac{45}{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the Length \\( QS \\):**\n   Since \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\), we know that \\( R \\) is one of the angles in a right triangle where one of the sides is 9 and the adjacent side is 4. We can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\( SR \\):\n   \\[\n   QR^2 + SR^2 = QS^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   9^2 + \\left( \\frac{45}{4} \\right)^2 = QS^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   81 + \\frac{2025}{16} = QS^2\n   \\]\n   Convert 81 to a fraction with a denominator of 16:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1296}{16} + \\frac{2025}{16} = QS^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3321}{16} = QS^2\n   \\]\n   Taking the square root of both sides:\n   \\[\n   QS = \\sqrt{\\frac{3321}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3321}}{4}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the length \\( QS \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3321}}{4}}\n\\]",
